  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 PM EDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis/Forecast:\n\nShortwave troughing continues to exit slowly off to the east, taking \ncorridor of forcing and deep layer moisture convergence east along \nwith it. Large area of Canada originated high pressure building \nsouth into the northern Lakes, with attendant aggressive deep layer \ndrying and subsidence bringing mostly sunny skies to areas north of \nthe big bridge, and helping scatter out some of the overcast across \nnorthern lower. While nothing too extreme, system backside northeast \nwinds ushering in a seasonably chilly airmass across the Northwoods, \nwith current readings mostly in the lower to middle 70s.\n\nShortwave troughing will continue to exit off to our east, replaced \nby rather stout mid level ridging and its attendant surface \nreflection to kick off this work week. Central NOAM upper level flow \nregime does remain somewhat progressive, with next wave and cold \nfront to visit the area the middle of this week. Deterministic \nguidance and a vast majority of their ensemble suite still support \nbroad troughing dominating northern Great Lakes weather to end the \nweek on into the start of the weekend, with at least some support \nfor a more zonal flavor to the upper level flow regime developing \ntoward the end of this forecast period.\n\nPrimary Forecast Concerns:\n\nTemperature trends through the period and addressing periodic rain \nchances...especially those on Wednesday.\n\nDetails: \n\nReally not a whole lot to discuss into Tuesday as vertically stacked \nhigh pressure controls our weather. Skies continue to become mostly \nclear tonight, and with some lingering low level moisture and winds \nbecoming light/calm, will likely allow some patchy fog to develop... \nespecially in more open areas and over/near inland bodies of water. \nTemperatures tonight will remain a bit chilly, with some of our \ntraditional colder interior areas likely making a run into the mid \nand upper 40s. Just about ideal early August weather for Monday and \nTuesday, with sun-filled skies and mild to warm weather. Simple \nairmass modification will help temperatures Monday reach near normal \nlevels in the upper 70s to lower 80s. Developing return flow, \ncoupled with that sunshine, will help tack on a few more degrees for \nhighs on Tuesday...when widespread afternoon temperatures in the \nlower to middle 80s are expected.\n\nWeather turns a bit more active Tuesday night and especially \nWednesday as shortwave troughing and its attendant cold front work \ninto and through the area...both collocated nicely with rather \nimpressive right entrance region upper jet dynamics. Expect at least \na broken line(s) of convection to attend this wave, with potential \nfor some diurnally-driven instability support Wednesday afternoon \nhelping both coverage and intensity of showers/storms (dependent on \nstill highly uncertain timing of course). Not tentatively seeing any \nreal support for any significant severe weather or heavy rain \nthreat, although cannot completely rule out a stronger storm or two \nwith isolated pockets of heavy rain given modest convective depth \nwind shear and precipitable water values up and over an inch and a \nhalf. Weather Prediction Center does highlight marginal risk wording \nfor excessive rains on Wednesday, specifically mentioning those \nimpressive jet dynamics. Plenty of time to work those details out in \nthe coming days.\n\nRest of the week currently looks rather uneventful, although suppose \na few showers are possible at times with any passing hard to time \nwaves within that broad overhead troughing. Temperatures will fall \nwell within the range of what is considered normal as we head into \nthe second week of August.",
